{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3101","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3101","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3101\",\"parent_revision\":\"026b75be2a122dadc13ecaaf049760c02dc0112e516aef83acf3d27dcc603774\",\"root_revision\":\"026b75be2a122dadc13ecaaf049760c02dc0112e516aef83acf3d27dcc603774\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: Ideas\\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-21\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nThis is an exploration/experiment item. The deliverable is an experiment result or design decision with evidence (a prototype on a real plan, a measured comparison, or a written design verdict) \u2014 not a mandate to ship the feature. Origin: comparison of graph-engineering research against the development-harness architecture, grounded against the #3060 agent over-orchestration incident (a task-worker recursively dispatched sub-teams after receiving orchestrator-voiced prose; ~$100 of unplanned spend). Shared design constraint across all seven exploration items in this set: the model keeps the judgment (scoring, declaring, deciding semantic dependencies) but writes it down ONCE, as typed data, at planning time; the mechanical consequence is applied deterministically by machinery that already exists (sam_schema models, DependencyGraph, the implement-feature dispatch loop, work-milestone). No new scheduler, no generic graph runtime \u2014 that larger direction is explicitly out of scope for these items.\\n\\nWhat/why: Milestone execution uses barrier scheduling: wave N+1 starts only when all of wave N completes, so the fastest item in a wave waits on the slowest. But WaveItem already carries depends_on[] \u2014 the waves are a derived view over a dependency graph that already exists in the DispatchPlan. Graph-engineering practice prefers per-node readiness (dispatch when YOUR dependencies are satisfied) over barriers unless a step genuinely needs cross-item context from the whole previous stage.\\n\\nMitigates: wall-clock waste from barrier synchronization; idle time between an item's dependencies merging and its wave formally opening.\\n\\nBenefit lands in: work-milestone execution time. No new data structures \u2014 this is a change to how the existing DispatchPlan is consumed.\\n\\nProposed exploration: (1) identify which wave boundaries in work-milestone genuinely need barrier semantics (e.g., a merge/relay step that reads ALL wave outputs) versus which are incidental; (2) prototype dispatching an item when its own depends_on entries are merged; (3) confirm conflict groups (shared-file mutation serialization) still hold under readiness-based ordering \u2014 conflict-group membership must remain a dispatch constraint independent of waves; (4) compare wall-clock on one milestone against the barrier baseline.\\n\\nRelated: Item 2 (\\\"Explore typed handoff payloads so task edges carry data flow, not just eligibility\\\") \u2014 the wave relay becomes a typed barrier node where a true barrier is retained.\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Experiment or design exploration executed as described (prototype on a real plan, measured comparison, or written design verdict)\\n- [ ] Decision recorded with evidence: adopt, adapt, or reject \u2014 with rationale\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer using Claude Code skills**, I want to **explore readiness-based dispatch in work-milestone, replacing whole-wave barriers** so that **the tooling becomes more capable and complete**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: graph-engineering research comparison\\n- **Priority**: Ideas\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-21\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"ee608c5446d62f8feea99939930c31c9b02b833c2eb43cf83bd4ec7b0d06c990\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABQIo6lA\"}"}